{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Keeping lovingkindness for thousands.</b> He preserves the merit of the fathers for the children, to do good to the children for the sake of their fathers." "<b>Bearing iniquity.</b> Deliberate [sin]." "<b>And transgression.</b> Rebellion against the kingdom." "<b>And sin.</b> Provocation added to the rebellion, as in כִּי רָעָתְכִי אָז תַּעֲלֹזִי (Jeremiah 11:15). But the bearing of one is not like the bearing of the other without doubt, and therefore they are enumerated as distinct attributes." "<b>And clearing.</b> Even though He clears the penitents out of love — which is repentance that reaches the throne of glory, as they said of blessed memory (Yoma 86a) that transgressions are accounted for them as merits, as it says of them: חָיֹה יִחְיֶה (Ezekiel 18:28) — nevertheless He does not completely clear even the penitents when their repentance is from fear of punishment alone; as they said of blessed memory that willful sins are accounted for them as inadvertencies, as it says: כִּי כָשַׁלְתָּ בַּעֲוֹנֶךָ (Hosea 14:2)." "<b>Visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children.</b> He waits to destroy the wicked of the earth until their measure is full, and this description means that their iniquity has reached a degree of evil beyond which there is no hope of repentance; and this will occur for the most part when their wickedness persists across several generations, one after the other." "<b>Upon the third generation.</b> When the latest generation adds to the evil of its fathers, as in וַיַּקְשׁוּ אֶת עׇרְפָּם, הֵרֵעוּ מֵאֲבוֹתָם (Jeremiah 7:26)." "<b>And upon the fourth.</b> When they do not add, but persist."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:28:05.255585037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}